hexagon: compute the matmul VTCM layout in 64 bits and saturate its int32 size
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-host

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The HVX matmul layout (htp_mm_hvx_vtcm_layout_build) multiplies the size of
an activation row by the row count, and the size of a weight row by the
prefetch depth and by the thread count, in size_t. size_t has 32 bits on
the DSP, thus a layout of 4 GB or more wraps, and the wrapped size can fit
the VTCM. The host keeps the size in the int32 field vtcm_size of the kernel
params, and the truncation gives the same small size. For example, a
MUL_MAT_ID with Q8_0 weights of 11648 columns and 10240 activation rows
needs 4296574464 bytes, and the two sides got 1607168 bytes. The DSP then
accepted the op and wrote beyond the VTCM.

This patch computes the sizes of that layout in uint64_t and saturates each
size of the layout struct to SIZE_MAX. The host stores the total in
vtcm_size with a saturation to INT32_MAX. Thus the host and the DSP refuse
such a layout. A layout below 4 GB gives the same sizes as before.

